  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Saturday: A broad low stretches and shifts \neast, before a secondary low drops in along the spine of the \nnorthern Rockies and skimming by the eastern CWA. Look for \ndecreasing precipitation chances this afternoon and evening, \nwith some fog development across the region. We will keep an eye\ntoward some locally dense fog around the Palouse and possibly \ntoward the Upper Columbia Basin for later tonight to Friday AM. \nThe secondary low will then increase over the east third of WA \nand ID Friday afternoon into Friday night, before warning into \nSaturday. That could lead to some light snow accumulations \naround 0.5 to 1\" over the central Pahandle Mountains, including\nLookout Pass. However the timing of the snow may limited \nimpacts. Expect lows in the 20s and low 30s. Highs will be in \nthe upper 30s and 40s. \n\nSaturday night to Sunday: The next low pressure will be\napproaching Friday and start to makes its way onshore by\nSaturday night and Sunday. A boundary lifts in with moisture \nand impulses ride along it. THis will be coupled with some \nconditionally unstable lapse rates. Look for increasing \nprecipitation from the west Saturday evening, expanding to \ncentral and southeast WA to lower ID overnight and all of the \narea Sunday morning. Models are still coming together with \nprecipitation amounts, but the NBM seems underdone compared to \nall the other guidance. So I blended with the CONSALL and WPC \nQPF amounts to increase those amounts in the official forecast. \nWith colder air in place, this will be mostly in the form of \nsnow. The exception will the deeper basin and L-C Valley. \nOutside of there, there is about a 30-50% chance of 1\" of snow. \nSo this could be the first inch for some areas, including the \nSpokane Metro; but places toward Moses Lake, Pullman and \nLewiston have a much lower risk of 1\". The mountains have the \npotential a little more, about 2 to 4 inches including the \npasses. Overall precipitation chances wane Sunday night, but the\nnext system will already be on the approach. Lows will be in \nthe 20s to low 30s, with highs in the mid-30s to low 40s.\n\nMonday to Thursday: the region remains in an active pattern with\nseveral opportunities for precipitation through the week. A \ndeeper trough comes to the coast Monday, shifting the main \nprecipitation potential toward the Cascades before the series of\nwaves start inland Tuesday onward. That will bring chances of \nprecipitation each day. The eye will be toward the Thanksgiving \ntravel time over the mountain passes with the potential for some\nmoderate snow, especially the Cascades. The lowlands are \nforecast to see more a rain/snow threat and more limited snow. \nAs we get closer, we will be able to fine-tune precipitation \namounts; however some modest impacts could come to those Cascade\npasses between Tuesday and Thursday. Highs are forecast to be \nin the upper 30s to mid-40s, with lows in the mid-20s to \nmid-30s. /Solveig",
